WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.560
Narrator: No one likes
finding bugs in their food

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.163
when they're not expecting it.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.070
But I hate to break it to you,

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.270
you're actually eating them all the time.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.210
I'm not just talking about the critters

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.830
that end up in juices or jams by accident.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.840
Some bugs are in our food because, well,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.143
we put them there.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.060
If you think it's fruit

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.180
that turns this strawberry
yogurt red, think again.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.900
Yes, there are in fact
strawberries in there,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.430
but they're there for flavor
and texture, not color.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.183
That bright red comes from
something else called carmine.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.670
Oh, and it's made from squashed bugs.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.250
Squashed female cochineal
bugs, to be specific.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.700
They're tick-sized critters
native to Mesoamerica

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.380
where they suck the juice
from prickly pear cactuses.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.300
Greig: And if you squish them,
they are bright red inside

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.810
and kind of a purply,
deep purple-red color,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.180
and that's the source of cochineal,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.280
cochineal dye, carmine,
whatever you wanna call it.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.890
Narrator: For thousands of
years, people have been using

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.070
these bugs to dye everything
from clothes to pottery.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.760
But it wasn't until more recently

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.120
that they made their way
into commercial foods.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.450
From 1955 to 2010, the
consumption of food coloring

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.130
rose by 500% in the United States.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.660
That's mostly thanks to artificial colors

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.010
like Red 40, Yellow 5, and Blue 1.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.830
But in the late 20th
century, consumers became

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.810
increasingly concerned
about synthetic chemicals

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.120
in their foods and demanded
more natural ingredients.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.350
So many companies turned to carmine.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.010
It's FDA improved and tasteless.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.620
It resists degradation from
light, heat, and oxidation,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.730
and unlike some synthetic colorants,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.630
it hasn't been linked to
cancers or tumor growth.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.460
Greig: Now, some people
apparently have allergies to it,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.370
but compared with the downsides

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.740
of the chemical dye, it's very benign.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.930
It's like using beet juice.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.270
Narrator: And just like
that, carmine ended up

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.770
in strawberry and cream Frappuccinos

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.060
and cake pops at Starbucks,

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.220
in Tropicana grapefruit juice,

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.350
and, yes, in Yoplait yogurts.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.280
Just look for carmine or cochineal extract

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.570
on the label to see for yourself.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.630
But today, carmine is becoming
harder and harder to come by.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.770
Some companies like Campari Group,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.590
maker of the famous Campari
aperitif, have phased it out

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.860
for economic reasons in the US.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.580
Greig: It's expensive to
make. I mean, it's easier

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.096
to just make a chemical dye,

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:49.870
and this is a very specialized,

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:51.320
you have to farm these little bugs,

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.590
and collect billions of pounds of them,

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.140
and dry all them and all that, so

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.250
I think that just wasn't as practical.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.380
Narrator: Other companies
took it out because, well,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:05.380
people still don't like eating
bugs, especially vegans.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.960
In 2012, a vegan news site

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.140
outed Starbucks for using cochineal

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.730
in its Strawberries and Crème Frappuccino,

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.600
saying that it's not vegan,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.760
and a month later, the
company said they'd switch

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.130
to a bug-free alternative.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.840
In fact, Yoplait now remains

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.530
one of the only major brands that sells

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.970
food colored with carmine.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:28.000
But even Yoplait may phase it out

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.900
after customers expressed concerns

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.690
about eating bug parts.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.120
Greig: So I just think it's ironic

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.810
people are freaked out about
insects, about eating insects,

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:37.710
even though we eat

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.760
2 pounds of insects a year
on average by accident.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.090
Narrator: And to show just
how harmless they are,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.173
we tried some.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.750
Jones: Nope, just tastes like yogurt!

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.540
Not buggy at all, in fact.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:54.970
Not that I would know what
that would taste like,

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:59.953
but I'm really just getting
plain Greek yogurt taste.

